1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,320
In 1939, Nazi Germany unleashed an awesome war machine.

2
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,520
As the most savage battle in history unfolded,

3
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:19,380
Nazi leaders forced German scientists to come up with an even weirder array of weapons.

4
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:25,640
Some were revolutionary, some were simply crazy.

5
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:29,760
All of them were designed to bring devastation to all corners of the globe,

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,140
and sustained the Third Reich's domination for a thousand years.

7
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:39,640
A global bomber that could fly through space at 13,000 miles an hour

8
00:00:39,641 --> 00:00:42,460
to bring destruction to every major city on the planet.

9
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:48,120
The world's first assault rifle that could shoot round corners.

10
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:55,180
A vortex gun designed to bring down an aircraft by creating a tornado.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,200
In the 1920s and 30s, rockets and the idea of space travel was all the rage in Germany.

12
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:19,440
Somehow, the idea had taken hold that one day soon, man would be able to travel to the moon.

13
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:22,420
Well, maybe not real soon.

14
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:29,140
Germany underwent a rocket craze in the 1920s.

15
00:01:29,141 --> 00:01:42,228
But behind the scenes, there was very serious work going on in an academic and engineering sense by people who really thought that we had a future based around rocket

16
00:01:42,229 --> 00:01:42,929
flight.

17
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:49,880
These pioneering scientists planned to develop all manner of rockets, planes, and spaceships.

18
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:55,120
Their vision included space travel to the moon, to Mars, and beyond.

19
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:58,700
Space stations from which they could control Earth's climate

20
00:01:58,701 --> 00:02:03,860
and rocket planes that could carry passengers around the Earth in around a couple of hours.

21
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:09,200
Their sole purpose was the exploration of space.

22
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:14,640
They were not expecting to see their revolutionary ideas used as weapons of war.

23
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:20,540
But the Nazis saw other possibilities.

24
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:24,300
They were quick to realize that rockets had military potential

25
00:02:24,301 --> 00:02:28,780
and offered limitless opportunities for a country bent on world domination.

26
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:34,500
German army generals like Walter Dornberger

27
00:02:34,501 --> 00:02:38,180
kept a close eye on the work of the amateur rocketeers.

28
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,180
When the Nazis came to power in 1933,

29
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,500
Dornberger hoped that by exploiting the genius

30
00:02:45,501 --> 00:02:48,520
of this exciting new generation of scientists and engineers,

31
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,160
the German army would be able to develop powerful and extraordinary new weapons.

32
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:56,000
He cherry-picked some of the best brains.

33
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,120
Top of the list was Werner von Braun.

34
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:03,540
Dornberger installed him at a new top-secret rocket research center

35
00:03:03,541 --> 00:03:05,980
at Pinemunde on Germany's Baltic coast.

36
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:10,840
Von Braun set to work.

37
00:03:10,841 --> 00:03:24,841
With limitless funds, Von Braun built rocket after rocket, not to travel to the moon, but to develop the world's first intercontinental ballistic missile.

38
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:42,880
Meanwhile, the man widely regarded as the founding father of German rocketry,

39
00:03:42,881 --> 00:03:47,420
Hermann Obert, had even more ambitious and fantastic ideas.

40
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:52,740
Obert believed that it was possible to colonize space.

41
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,760
He proposed an orbiting space station.

42
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,660
He also planned to fire a giant mirror into space

43
00:03:59,661 --> 00:04:03,040
to reflect great beams of sunlight onto dark parts of the Earth.

44
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:07,680
Originally, these were ideas of peace,

45
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,260
but now he offered them as weapons to aid the Nazi war effort.

46
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:14,860
He had almost like a dual personality,

47
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:18,340
and one side of it, in addition to being a pacifist, he was a warrior.

48
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:23,360
He also said my space mirror could also be used for military purposes.

49
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:25,240
I could burn up ships at sea.

50
00:04:25,241 --> 00:04:31,860
I could focus my space mirror on troop concentrations and destroy them.

51
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:37,780
I could focus in on cities and burn large holes in places like London or New York City.

52
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:42,740
But Oberth's ideas were far in advance of their time.

53
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:45,540
Although he worked for the Nazis at Penemunda,

54
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:49,240
his space wheel and mirror were seen as wonder weapons for the future

55
00:04:49,241 --> 00:04:51,700
when the Nazis had won the war on Earth.

56
00:04:55,240 --> 00:05:00,240
But by 1943, thoughts of world domination were receding fast.

57
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:02,320
The war was changing course.

58
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:07,920
Wave upon wave of American B-17 bombers were decimating German cities.

59
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:13,340
Privately, many German leaders were beginning to fear that this was a war they could not win.

60
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:18,300
Among them, Hermann Göring, head of the German Luftwaffe.

61
00:05:18,301 --> 00:05:27,160
Herman Goering realized by 1943 that there was a definite possibility that Germany would lose this war.

62
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:31,240
The American mass production of B-17 bombers was overwhelming.

63
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:36,420
If something was not done to hit the sources of mass production, Germany would in fact lose the war.

64
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:42,280
So now, Goering turned to the rocket engineers for inspiration.

65
00:05:42,281 --> 00:05:50,040
A brilliant scientist called Eugen Sanger had an idea for a plane that could travel through space.

66
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:57,300
If you want an example of looking really into the future, then look at the amazing designs of Eugen Sanger.

67
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:01,220
This man had a plan for what he called an antipodal bomber.

68
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:03,560
It could fly to the Antipodes, the other side of the world.

69
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:11,280
Sanger's space bomber was decades ahead of its time.

70
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:24,220
At takeoff, the plane was to be propelled along a monorail by a sled fired by V2 rockets.

71
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:32,200
After 1.7 miles, the plane would be traveling at over 1,000 miles an hour.

72
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:42,160
As it left the track, a second rocket inside the bomber would alter the plane's trajectory

73
00:06:42,161 --> 00:06:45,120
and propel it 90 miles into space.

74
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:54,860
The point about Eugen Sanger's design was

75
00:06:54,861 --> 00:06:57,260
that you'd in essence put the plane into an orbit.

76
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:00,140
So you'd burn a lot of fuel to get it up there,

77
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:02,100
but once it was on the fringe of space,

78
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:04,480
it could then act more or less as a glider.

79
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:07,200
It could then come back and intersect

80
00:07:07,201 --> 00:07:10,140
with the upper layers of the atmosphere

81
00:07:10,141 --> 00:07:13,920
and then use them like a pebble skipping on a lake.

82
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:18,320
A pilot would fly the plane along the edge of the Earth's atmosphere.

83
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:24,900
Traveling at 13,500 miles an hour, it would circuit the Earth in two hours.

84
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:29,820
It could drop its lethal payload on any city in the world.

85
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:33,420
Although the Germans had no nuclear bomb capability,

86
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:39,020
they planned to use a bomb that would spread radioactive particles into the atmosphere.

87
00:07:40,140 --> 00:07:46,060
The bomb carried by the Sanger bomber would have been a 5,000-pound high-explosive bomb

88
00:07:46,061 --> 00:07:48,340
wrapped with blankets of radioactive silica.

89
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:50,780
The device would be dropped.

90
00:07:52,140 --> 00:07:55,680
At about 2,000 feet, 3,000 feet above the city, it would explode.

91
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:04,240
The radioactive silica would fall like snowflakes,

92
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:09,720
and the end result would be radioactive sickness and death.

93
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:20,080
Having dropped its deadly payload, the space bomber would glide back to Germany, gradually re-entering Earth's atmosphere.

94
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:31,440
The landing part, where it's in essence gliding down to Earth without any power, is very much like the shuttle.

95
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:38,040
The difference between the antipodal bomber and the modern shuttle is that the shuttle doesn't have engines, and the antipodal bomber did.

96
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:41,620
This was clearly a revolutionary weapon,

97
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:44,540
which really could change the course of the war.

98
00:08:50,020 --> 00:08:54,360
Göring immediately set Sanger up in his own research base in northern Germany

99
00:08:54,361 --> 00:08:57,560
with instructions to develop the antipodal bomber.

100
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:02,200
But Sanger had a secret.

101
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,880
He knew perfectly well that his idea would be years in the making.

102
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:10,780
But desperate for the funding, he omitted to tell Goering about the timescale.

103
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:17,280
He even persuaded Nazi leaders that the anti-Polo bomber could be a useful propaganda tool for them.

104
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:24,340
The Nazis were delighted because they had a new piece of armory in their propaganda machine.

105
00:09:24,780 --> 00:09:28,440
Eugen Sanger was delighted because somebody was paying him to indulge his favorite hobby.

106
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:35,720
When Hermann Goering finally realized the anti-Polo bomber was an idea that was simply too far ahead of its time,

107
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:38,060
His mood abruptly changed.

108
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:47,260
Zenger, I think, was largely lucky to avoid simply being thrown into some sort of camp or prison

109
00:09:47,261 --> 00:09:52,280
for what was increasingly seen by the Germans as a waste of their wartime resources and money.

110
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:59,820
After the war, Zenger's report on his antipodal bomber was carefully analyzed by the victorious Allies.

111
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:04,760
Some of its features, like the system of re-entry into Earth's atmosphere,

112
00:10:04,761 --> 00:10:06,720
can be seen in today's space shuttle.

113
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:13,280
There is one crucial difference.

114
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:16,060
The main difference, of course,

115
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:19,420
is that the space shuttle is carried aloft on a rocket,

116
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:23,340
whereas Eugenssanger's antipodal bomber

117
00:10:23,341 --> 00:10:25,320
would have got there under its own steam.

118
00:10:25,321 --> 00:10:29,240
In fact, of course, it wouldn't have worked.

119
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:31,520
You would need a multistage rocket

120
00:10:31,521 --> 00:10:35,900
to get something like that bomber up into near-Earth orbit.

121
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:40,480
It is impossible to put a heavy plane of that sort up into near-orbit

122
00:10:40,481 --> 00:10:42,340
simply by carrying its own fuel.

123
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:47,780
Nazi inventiveness and ambition knew no limits.

124
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:50,340
Backed by outstanding engineers,

125
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:53,500
they would produce many fantastic weird weapons of destruction

126
00:10:53,501 --> 00:10:54,400
that worked brilliantly,

127
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:57,340
like a gun that could shoot round corners.

128
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:08,880
Throughout history, German technology consistently produced guns of superb quality.

129
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:12,240
Not only were they bigger and better than anyone else's,

130
00:11:12,241 --> 00:11:15,520
they were more simply engineered and more efficiently produced.

131
00:11:16,780 --> 00:11:23,160
One of the best was the MP44, the world's first assault rifle.

132
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:30,600
The thing that makes this weapon really unique in 1944 is the fact that it's both semi-automatic and fully automatic.

133
00:11:30,601 --> 00:11:34,920
And by semi-automatic, I mean every time you pull the trigger back, one pressure, it fires one round.

134
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:39,600
But you can put this thing into full automatic fire, and when you do that thing,

135
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:43,120
when you pull back the trigger, it will continue to fire until the magazine is empty.

136
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:47,000
No one had a gun to compare to this.

137
00:11:47,580 --> 00:11:51,300
Standard issue for Allied troops was either a rifle or a submachine gun.

138
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:54,240
Both were at a disadvantage against the assault rifle.

139
00:11:56,300 --> 00:11:58,680
Then, German engineers went one better.

140
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:02,680
They came up with a thing called the curved barrel device.

141
00:12:03,260 --> 00:12:05,640
And here it is. It's a way to shoot around corners.

142
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:12,620
And what this is, this is a very, very heavy piece of metal that was stuck on the end of the barrel.

143
00:12:12,621 --> 00:12:21,220
And as the bullet came down here and hit this, it was turned 90 degrees and exited here.

144
00:12:21,780 --> 00:12:26,700
The curved barrel was developed because of a rare engineering error on a tank assembly line.

145
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:31,380
Engineers found they had built tanks which could not be fitted with the normal turrets.

146
00:12:32,140 --> 00:12:36,940
Ever resourceful, they gave the vehicle flat roof and a new name, an elephant.

147
00:12:39,320 --> 00:12:45,140
They fitted it with an 88-millimeter gun to use as a long-range weapon against Russian tanks on the eastern front.

148
00:12:45,900 --> 00:12:52,180
But in their haste, someone forgot to fit a machine gun, leaving the elephant fatally exposed to attack.

149
00:12:52,181 --> 00:12:57,700
Russian infantry learned real quick that they could run up to these vehicles,

150
00:12:57,701 --> 00:13:00,620
pour gasoline in the top, and torch the vehicle.

151
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:03,840
And the vehicles could not support each other.

152
00:13:04,060 --> 00:13:05,440
You don't have a machine gun aboard the vehicle.

153
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:09,420
So if you're here and I'm here, Russian infantry is climbing on your vehicle.

154
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:12,700
I have no way with a machine gun to keep them off.

155
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:19,580
A weapon was needed to help the German crews to defend themselves inside the elephants.

156
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:33,315
So the modified MP44 with this curved barrel device was created so that the German crew could stick this outside the turret and hose the vehicle down to keep Russian

157
00:13:33,316 --> 00:13:35,880
infantry from climbing on the vehicle.

158
00:13:37,220 --> 00:13:39,220
Engineers experimented with two models.

159
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:42,120
One curved 30 degrees the other by 90.

160
00:13:43,100 --> 00:13:46,640
The sharper angle was for use in confined spaces like tanks.

161
00:13:46,641 --> 00:13:51,580
having made a gun that could shoot around corners

162
00:13:51,581 --> 00:13:54,880
now German troops wanted to see who they were firing at

163
00:13:54,881 --> 00:14:03,220
you'll notice that it has an area here that's sort of cut away and it's rough

164
00:14:03,221 --> 00:14:05,780
and what the Germans installed there was a mirror

165
00:14:05,781 --> 00:14:14,480
so that they had the sight back here and they could look around the corner to see what they were shooting at

166
00:14:14,481 --> 00:14:16,840
There was one more small problem.

167
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:20,600
The curved barrel tended to distort and shatter bullets as they were fired.

168
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:25,240
But even that turned out to be an advantage for soldiers fighting at close quarters.

169
00:14:28,180 --> 00:14:35,900
As the bullet's traveling around the barrel, the bullet is going to heat up tremendously and basically is going to fly apart.

170
00:14:38,060 --> 00:14:42,880
So what you're going to get is a shotgun effect coming out of the muzzle of the weapon.

171
00:14:42,881 --> 00:14:47,880
But remember, you're fighting at very, very close ranges, so that doesn't really matter.

172
00:14:47,881 --> 00:14:52,880
With or without the curved barrel, the MP44 seemed to have everything.

173
00:14:52,881 --> 00:14:57,880
Yet strangely, there was one man in Germany who didn't like it, Adolf Hitler.

174
00:14:57,881 --> 00:15:03,880
When he was first shown this thing, he hated it because it was made out of stampings, and

175
00:15:03,881 --> 00:15:05,880
it looked ugly to him.

176
00:15:05,881 --> 00:15:07,880
So he said, don't produce it.

177
00:15:07,881 --> 00:15:09,880
Well, the German army did it anyway.

178
00:15:09,881 --> 00:15:13,100
him, which was not a good thing to do if you were a German general.

179
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:17,340
Well, they got away with it because this was such a good weapon.

180
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:22,400
German engineers set the highest standards of innovative gun design.

181
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:29,280
But in 1944, as the war was slipping away, they began to experiment with ever more bizarre

182
00:15:29,281 --> 00:15:29,981
weapons.

183
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:36,980
Guns that astonishingly didn't fire bullets or shells, but instead used elements of nature

184
00:15:36,981 --> 00:15:38,020
as ammunition.

185
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:42,560
These weapons were the brainchild of Albert Speer,

186
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:43,960
Hitler's armaments minister.

187
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:48,380
He set up research bases in Germany and Austria to develop them.

188
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:52,400
This was one.

189
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:55,320
It may look like two giant loudspeakers,

190
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:59,080
but in fact it was a sound gun designed to kill.

191
00:16:02,180 --> 00:16:05,580
The gun worked by igniting a mixture of methane and oxygen

192
00:16:05,581 --> 00:16:06,640
in a resonant chamber.

193
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:11,280
This created a chain of explosions, up to 1,500 a second,

194
00:16:11,740 --> 00:16:14,360
which set down a powerful and focused beam of sound,

195
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:17,240
which was amplified by parabolic reflectors.

196
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:20,680
The pressure of the sound was unendurable.

197
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:30,000
It was the same as being plunged 30 feet beneath the sea in terms of pressure,

198
00:16:30,001 --> 00:16:34,440
and yet that pressure being reversed hundreds of times every second.

199
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:42,600
It was a vibrating sound which could penetrate the human body,

200
00:16:42,601 --> 00:16:46,880
and the idea was you could disrupt the brain, disrupt your heart.

201
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:51,060
German records estimated that the sound gun would take half a minute

202
00:16:51,061 --> 00:16:53,680
to kill a man standing within 100 yards

203
00:16:53,681 --> 00:16:57,960
and seriously injure anyone within 300 yards.

204
00:16:59,340 --> 00:17:03,680
The sound gun may have been lethal, but it was also big and cumbersome.

205
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:05,980
It was never used in battle.

206
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:10,680
But I'm sure those practical problems could have been overcome.

207
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:14,560
And somewhere in the world, I'll bet there's a crazed dictator

208
00:17:14,561 --> 00:17:16,560
who's longing to revive the idea.

209
00:17:18,260 --> 00:17:20,400
As well as using sound as a weapon,

210
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:24,700
Nazi engineers conducted tests with guns that fired air.

211
00:17:25,660 --> 00:17:29,240
And the idea was that inside it you'd fuel it with hydrogen and oxygen,

212
00:17:29,560 --> 00:17:31,500
which would cause a tremendous explosion,

213
00:17:31,501 --> 00:17:35,580
and out would come a plug of air, an invisible plug of compressed air,

214
00:17:35,581 --> 00:17:39,000
that could bring down an aircraft flying about.

215
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:41,940
The wind gun was used just once

216
00:17:41,941 --> 00:17:45,820
when the Nazis tried unsuccessfully to defend a bridge on the Elbe River.

217
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:49,520
No planes were shot down, and the weapon was abandoned.

218
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:53,960
None of these extraordinary weapons survive today.

219
00:17:55,860 --> 00:17:58,780
But in California, an entertainment group

220
00:17:58,781 --> 00:18:02,420
have painstakingly recreated some weird Nazi guns from World War II

221
00:18:02,421 --> 00:18:04,280
for the mechanization display.

222
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:10,420
The idea is to give audiences a feel for what it was like in war.

223
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:17,340
To feel a concussion of something like that hitting you,

224
00:18:17,520 --> 00:18:22,140
and the fire and the explosions and the smell of diesel

225
00:18:22,141 --> 00:18:25,000
and dirt being kicked up and all that

226
00:18:25,001 --> 00:18:27,960
is the closest that you're ever going to get

227
00:18:27,961 --> 00:18:29,980
to actually being in a battle without being there.

228
00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:35,160
In this show, the guns are harmless.

229
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:38,720
They make a lot of noise and bring down model houses.

230
00:18:40,460 --> 00:18:44,720
But they were built using the same principles as the Nazis over 60 years ago.

231
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:51,460
Of course, the original designs were the designs that the Germans made in World War II.

232
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:58,560
They're the people, they're the granddaddies of all this kind of work with vortex guns and sound weapons and things like that.

233
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:02,200
This is a scaled-down vortex gun or cannon.

234
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:05,980
The original German version was around 20 times bigger.

235
00:19:06,680 --> 00:19:09,780
The German gun was designed to create a vortex or whirlwind

236
00:19:09,781 --> 00:19:12,000
strong enough to rip the wings from an aircraft.

237
00:19:14,580 --> 00:19:18,540
This cannon, although much smaller, works on similar principles.

238
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:21,660
An explosive charge is placed inside the cannon.

239
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:27,560
Upon ignition, an invisible vortex is fired into the air at 100 miles an hour.

240
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:36,820
Well, this one is harmless enough.

241
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:40,140
It makes a lot of noise and can shatter a pane of glass.

242
00:19:40,280 --> 00:19:40,980
It did?

243
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:43,820
But the Germans not only had a much bigger gun,

244
00:19:44,100 --> 00:19:47,280
they added a special ingredient, powdered coal dust,

245
00:19:47,660 --> 00:19:49,800
which, when mixed with the exploding vortex,

246
00:19:50,260 --> 00:19:51,760
made it far more powerful.

247
00:19:55,260 --> 00:19:59,820
It was known for centuries that powders in air will explode.

248
00:20:00,660 --> 00:20:03,260
Flour in a flour mill can suddenly explode.

249
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,340
Coal dust, coal powder in a mine can suddenly explode.

250
00:20:10,180 --> 00:20:12,720
And it was an Austrian invention during the war

251
00:20:12,721 --> 00:20:16,340
to try and harness this process to create a vortex gun.

252
00:20:17,180 --> 00:20:21,160
The reaction of the coal dust with air made the vortex bigger and bigger

253
00:20:21,161 --> 00:20:24,000
until it became the size of a small whirlwind.

254
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:29,680
The dust would continue burning, fueling the spiral as it goes,

255
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:34,560
and in theory at least, setting off an enormous great whirlwind

256
00:20:34,561 --> 00:20:36,520
that was enough to bring down a plane.

257
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,300
Like the sound gun and the wind gun,

258
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:44,020
the Vortex gun never quite made it on the battlefield.

259
00:20:44,680 --> 00:20:48,220
The idea was truly extraordinary, but could it have worked?

260
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,060
Could it have ever brought down a plane?

261
00:20:52,540 --> 00:20:54,640
If the planes had cooperated, yeah.

262
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:57,120
I mean, from what they knew and what they were able to do,

263
00:20:57,160 --> 00:20:59,580
it's pretty amazing that they built these things,

264
00:20:59,581 --> 00:21:01,760
and they got the effects they did out of them.

265
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:04,140
But I think from a practical standpoint,

266
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:07,700
it would have been a lucky shot, the proverbial lucky shot.

267
00:21:10,100 --> 00:21:13,520
But other ideas for weird weapons were far more successful.

268
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:18,060
Early in the war, the Nazis were harnessing nature

269
00:21:18,061 --> 00:21:19,340
in a quite different way,

270
00:21:20,060 --> 00:21:22,400
developing tanks that could travel under the sea

271
00:21:22,401 --> 00:21:25,780
and giant machines to fly the men and machinery they needed

272
00:21:25,781 --> 00:21:27,600
to invade England.

273
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:35,660
June 1940.

274
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:40,200
In just a few short months, the German army had conquered most of Europe.

275
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:48,000
While Hitler was sightseeing in Paris, his air force, the Luftwaffe,

276
00:21:48,360 --> 00:21:51,900
had orders to soften up the British to pave the way for German invasion.

277
00:21:55,240 --> 00:21:59,020
The success of the German army was based on blitzkrieg tactics.

278
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:04,480
The sheer speed and surprise of the ferocious attacks left defenders helpless.

279
00:22:05,720 --> 00:22:07,720
Britain posed a new challenge, though.

280
00:22:08,260 --> 00:22:13,780
Hitler had to figure out a way of launching a surprise attack across the 26 miles of the English Channel.

281
00:22:14,380 --> 00:22:20,840
He wanted to get an invasion force of 160,000 men with heavy armor onto the shores of England.

282
00:22:21,480 --> 00:22:23,640
Fast and with maximum shock.

283
00:22:24,320 --> 00:22:28,580
Germany had invested heavily in assault gliders in the 1930s,

284
00:22:28,581 --> 00:22:31,600
and these were used very successfully in 1940

285
00:22:31,601 --> 00:22:33,640
during the invasion across the Low Countries.

286
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:35,740
But these were all very small gliders

287
00:22:35,741 --> 00:22:39,960
capable of carrying at most 10 or 15 or perhaps 20 troops.

288
00:22:40,600 --> 00:22:42,560
Hitler asked the Messerschmitt factory

289
00:22:42,561 --> 00:22:45,360
to come up with a design for something much bigger.

290
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:48,760
Amazingly, within 14 days,

291
00:22:48,880 --> 00:22:51,380
they produced drawings for the largest glider in history.

292
00:22:51,920 --> 00:22:55,400
It would carry nearly 10 times more than any other German glider.

293
00:22:56,100 --> 00:22:57,460
Hitler wanted it immediately.

294
00:22:58,220 --> 00:23:00,400
He planned to invade England in weeks.

295
00:23:01,960 --> 00:23:03,840
Messerschmitt knew they had to build it fast.

296
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:07,520
Construction began on June 11, 1940.

297
00:23:08,380 --> 00:23:09,960
Prisoners of war were ordered to help.

298
00:23:11,080 --> 00:23:13,600
Local factories were enlisted to provide materials.

299
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:19,980
The aircraft was called a Gigant, and it was an astonishing size.

300
00:23:20,120 --> 00:23:26,180
The wingspan was 180 feet, and its fuselage would be nearly 100 feet in length.

301
00:23:27,460 --> 00:23:33,760
To keep the airframe light, technicians developed a unique formula of wooden steel.

302
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:40,740
The main structure comprised hollow steel tubing fortified by ribs, 22 on each wing.

303
00:23:42,740 --> 00:23:45,740
They were shaped and supplied by a local furniture factory.

304
00:23:47,620 --> 00:23:52,240
A bed factory produced the steel frame for the massive loading doors at the front of the plane.

305
00:23:52,241 --> 00:23:59,920
Hitler had asked for a plane that could fly a tank through the air

306
00:23:59,921 --> 00:24:02,920
Messerschmitt built something even bigger

307
00:24:02,921 --> 00:24:10,920
The new glider could hold 120 fully equipped troops

308
00:24:10,921 --> 00:24:14,180
In all, it could carry a load of 24 tons

309
00:24:14,181 --> 00:24:19,200
Incredibly, just three sheets of reinforced plywood would take the weight

310
00:24:19,201 --> 00:24:25,440
plywood where you stick together sheets of wood with the grain at opposite directions

311
00:24:25,441 --> 00:24:32,760
is much stronger than normal wood is and if you impregnate it soak it with resin or with some sort

312
00:24:32,761 --> 00:24:40,500
of varnish then it becomes much much stronger the next big challenge was to get the gigant into the

313
00:24:40,501 --> 00:24:46,560
air meanwhile another weird idea has been developed for the seabed a key element of

314
00:24:46,561 --> 00:24:48,390
Hitler's invasion plans was tanks.

315
00:24:48,391 --> 00:24:51,620
To keep the element of surprise and get them into the battle

316
00:24:51,621 --> 00:24:57,468
quickly, why not drive them ashore, underwater?

317
00:24:57,469 --> 00:25:03,440
From July 1940, 168 Talcpanzers were modified so

318
00:25:03,441 --> 00:25:06,088
they could operate below the surface of the sea.

319
00:25:06,089 --> 00:25:08,680
They were sealed up and snorkels were fitted to

320
00:25:08,681 --> 00:25:13,805
provide air for the crew. They were called dipping or deep-weighting tanks.

321
00:25:13,806 --> 00:25:15,240
The main problem of a

322
00:25:15,241 --> 00:25:19,480
deep-weighting tank that's actually all the way underwater and simply using a snorkel is that

323
00:25:19,481 --> 00:25:21,245
they're still somewhat buoyant.

324
00:25:21,246 --> 00:25:24,660
It might seem strange that a 25-ton steel object is buoyant,

325
00:25:24,740 --> 00:25:28,300
but it still has air in it, and as a result, it doesn't have good traction underwater.

326
00:25:30,280 --> 00:25:33,000
Tests were carried out at Kutlos on the Baltic coast.

327
00:25:36,260 --> 00:25:40,700
They found that the tanks often became bogged down on soft soil like a riverbed.

328
00:25:40,701 --> 00:25:49,360
for the crews trapped 45 feet underwater escape was a terrifying ordeal the hatches basically

329
00:25:49,361 --> 00:25:53,760
have all of the weight of the water on top of them so basically you have to let the tank fill

330
00:25:53,761 --> 00:25:58,340
with water you can then open up the hatch and escape using an aqualung or something similar

331
00:25:58,341 --> 00:26:02,560
but the problem is for the five or six minutes that it's taking to fill with water it's pretty

332
00:26:02,561 --> 00:26:08,280
terrifying while tank crews were learning how to escape from a tank stuck underwater

333
00:26:08,281 --> 00:26:14,980
Messerschmitt's engineers were puzzling how to get the biggest glider on Earth into the air.

334
00:26:17,740 --> 00:26:21,020
An ordinary-sized glider was towed by another aircraft.

335
00:26:22,580 --> 00:26:26,180
But for the Gigant, there was no available plane that was big enough.

336
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:32,720
Rockets were attached to the wing to give more lift to takeoff, but it wasn't enough.

337
00:26:32,721 --> 00:26:38,980
Next, they harnessed it to three separate aircraft to pull this monster into the air

338
00:26:38,981 --> 00:26:43,980
Sometimes it worked, sometimes it didn't

339
00:26:43,981 --> 00:26:49,160
To coordinate three of them was impossibly difficult

340
00:26:49,161 --> 00:26:51,800
They had endless crashes and near crashes

341
00:26:51,801 --> 00:26:58,100
And it became obvious that using three separate aircraft to tow just one was never going to work reliably

342
00:26:58,101 --> 00:27:02,860
The next idea was simply bizarre.

343
00:27:03,960 --> 00:27:08,780
Take two planes, cut them in half, add an extra engine, and fuse them back together.

344
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:11,920
It was called a Heinkel 111Z.

345
00:27:12,440 --> 00:27:16,760
And although it looked decidedly odd, it worked.

346
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:24,240
Eventually, someone had the bright idea of fitting the glider with its own engines.

347
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:29,820
Six French gnome-rown Butte engines turned the Gigant from the world's largest glider

348
00:27:29,821 --> 00:27:32,020
into the world's greatest transport plane.

349
00:27:32,940 --> 00:27:35,640
It would make more noise, but it would do the job.

350
00:27:39,740 --> 00:27:43,480
The underwater tanks and giant gliders were strange ideas.

351
00:27:43,960 --> 00:27:47,580
But both were available to spearhead the invasion of Britain.

352
00:27:48,920 --> 00:27:53,920
But determined resistance from the British Air Force persuaded Hitler to think again.

353
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:59,340
In September 1940, the invasion of Britain was cancelled.

354
00:28:02,040 --> 00:28:08,480
But the flying gigant and the dipping tanks played their part when Hitler came to invade Russia in 1941.

355
00:28:13,160 --> 00:28:19,460
As the course of war swung back and forth, the German war machine came up with ever more inventive ideas.

356
00:28:20,120 --> 00:28:24,760
In 1944, the Nazis, using amazing new vertical takeoff planes,

357
00:28:25,100 --> 00:28:28,440
hit back at U.S. bombers who were decimating German cities.

358
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:37,360
At the outset of World War II, Germany had the finest air force in the world.

359
00:28:41,860 --> 00:28:45,800
The Junkers 88 bomber and the Messerschmitt 109 fighter

360
00:28:45,801 --> 00:28:48,200
were a match for any aircraft in the world.

361
00:28:49,460 --> 00:28:54,460
In 1940, Air Force Commander Gehring and Adolf Hitler

362
00:28:54,461 --> 00:28:58,460
were so confident of the invulnerability of the German Air Force,

363
00:28:58,461 --> 00:29:01,460
they put a break on aircraft development and research.

364
00:29:01,461 --> 00:29:04,460
They just didn't think they'd need it.

365
00:29:04,461 --> 00:29:08,460
It was a decision that would have dire consequences for Nazi Germany.

366
00:29:08,461 --> 00:29:14,460
Because, by 1944, aircraft like the Me 109 were practically obsolete.

367
00:29:14,461 --> 00:29:19,740
obsolete. They were no match for the new U.S. fighters like the Mustang.

368
00:29:23,740 --> 00:29:24,971
As a result, U.S.

369
00:29:24,972 --> 00:29:30,620
bombers enjoyed almost complete protection as they relentlessly pounded German

370
00:29:30,621 --> 00:29:31,321
targets.

371
00:29:31,322 --> 00:29:38,480
Hermann Göring and the German Air Force were desperate for a fantastic new aircraft that

372
00:29:38,481 --> 00:29:42,438
just might tilt the balance of the war back in Germany's favor.

373
00:29:42,439 --> 00:29:44,260
They were prepared to pay any

374
00:29:44,261 --> 00:29:45,441
price to get it.

375
00:29:45,442 --> 00:29:51,200
If you look at the list of Nazi developments as the war went on, the one thing

376
00:29:51,201 --> 00:29:54,223
that does strike you is how profligate they were in wasting money.

377
00:29:54,224 --> 00:29:55,460
And there's no other way of

378
00:29:55,461 --> 00:29:56,161
looking at it.

379
00:29:56,162 --> 00:30:00,340
I mean, they weren't developing an ideal fighter and making it bigger and better and

380
00:30:00,341 --> 00:30:03,832
faster. They were developing dozens of them.

381
00:30:03,833 --> 00:30:07,800
By 1944, the German Air Ministry needed a miracle.

382
00:30:09,400 --> 00:30:12,740
They hoped that the many ideas that the aircraft designers were now working on

383
00:30:12,741 --> 00:30:14,480
would help them to fight back.

384
00:30:16,780 --> 00:30:20,300
One urgent requirement was for a defense interceptor

385
00:30:20,301 --> 00:30:22,600
that could be inexpensively mass-produced.

386
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:25,280
There were many suggestions.

387
00:30:26,340 --> 00:30:27,440
One was called the Natter.

388
00:30:28,100 --> 00:30:30,220
It was a small piloted jet rocket.

389
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:34,500
Using cheap wood, it could be easily built

390
00:30:34,501 --> 00:30:36,960
in small workshops by semi-skilled laborers.

391
00:30:37,920 --> 00:30:39,440
Launched from a vertical pole,

392
00:30:39,441 --> 00:30:42,880
It was designed to fly at 600 miles an hour.

393
00:30:43,880 --> 00:30:50,060
This was a small, one-manned rocket that took off vertically at high speed,

394
00:30:50,620 --> 00:30:54,380
roared up into the upper regions of the atmosphere, and then the engine cut out.

395
00:30:55,200 --> 00:31:01,800
Then the pilot used it as a glider to attack aircraft as he very gently descended towards the Earth.

396
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:05,520
The Natter's firepower was awesome.

397
00:31:06,220 --> 00:31:09,440
It had 24 air-to-air rockets to fire from its nose.

398
00:31:10,200 --> 00:31:13,500
But if that failed, the pilots were encouraged to bail out

399
00:31:13,501 --> 00:31:16,500
and ram the plane itself into enemy aircraft.

400
00:31:20,020 --> 00:31:22,960
The natter itself was considered quite expendable.

401
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:26,380
If the falling plane didn't strike an Allied aircraft,

402
00:31:26,620 --> 00:31:28,200
it would be left to crash to Earth.

403
00:31:29,280 --> 00:31:32,240
Only the engine would be recovered for future use.

404
00:31:32,241 --> 00:31:36,920
The design was considered so weird and dangerous

405
00:31:36,921 --> 00:31:39,340
that the German Air Ministry rejected it.

406
00:31:40,180 --> 00:31:44,260
But the designer, Eric Bauckham, bypassed the conventional route.

407
00:31:44,940 --> 00:31:48,960
He persuaded Heinrich Himmler, leader of the SS, to fund the program.

408
00:31:51,220 --> 00:31:54,480
Despite Himmler's backing, it flew only once

409
00:31:54,481 --> 00:31:58,740
and crashed shortly after takeoff, killing the test pilot, Lothar Siebert.

410
00:31:58,741 --> 00:32:03,500
On the weird scale, the Natter comes near the top.

411
00:32:03,580 --> 00:32:05,900
It may not be the chart topper, but it can't be far from it.

412
00:32:07,580 --> 00:32:10,620
Almost as weird was another rocket-fired interceptor,

413
00:32:10,880 --> 00:32:13,420
this one from Messerschmitt, the ME-163.

414
00:32:15,500 --> 00:32:17,020
The plane took off normally,

415
00:32:17,520 --> 00:32:21,720
but was then blasted by a rocket vertically into the air at 600 miles an hour.

416
00:32:22,220 --> 00:32:23,920
This was an unheard-of idea.

417
00:32:23,921 --> 00:32:32,720
it was powered with a proper rocket engine and the idea was that it was launched on a set of

418
00:32:32,721 --> 00:32:37,600
trolley wheels that could fall away as it took off so it didn't have the bother and the aerodynamic

419
00:32:37,601 --> 00:32:42,160
encumbrance of actually carrying its own undercarriage then it would use its rocket power

420
00:32:42,161 --> 00:32:47,800
to fly up to 20 30 000 feet and then glide down shooting aircraft out of the sky as it came

421
00:32:47,801 --> 00:32:53,580
the fact that it launched vertically like a little space rocket gave it a great advantage

422
00:32:53,581 --> 00:32:56,340
must have been held to sit in, though, from the pilot's point of view.

423
00:33:01,760 --> 00:33:04,660
When the plane returned to Earth, it landed on a sled.

424
00:33:05,440 --> 00:33:08,900
Like the Natter, the 163 was incredibly dangerous to fly.

425
00:33:09,960 --> 00:33:11,840
Takeoff and landings were particularly hazardous.

426
00:33:13,060 --> 00:33:15,720
The rocket fuel was highly volatile and combustible.

427
00:33:16,040 --> 00:33:18,940
A tiny error when a pilot could cause an explosion.

428
00:33:18,941 --> 00:33:24,940
Of the pilots who died in the 163, just 5% were killed in combat.

429
00:33:26,780 --> 00:33:29,800
The rest lost their lives just trying to make it work.

430
00:33:32,760 --> 00:33:37,000
But where on the scale of weird does the Messerschmitt 163 rate?

431
00:33:37,400 --> 00:33:41,040
Well, not half as high up the scale as the Natter did.

432
00:33:41,220 --> 00:33:43,660
It was a success. It did work.

433
00:33:43,920 --> 00:33:45,360
But it was pretty crazy nonetheless.

434
00:33:45,361 --> 00:33:50,760
In spite of the human losses, the 163 could have been a success.

435
00:33:51,360 --> 00:33:54,840
But it didn't stem the relentless stream of U.S. bombers.

436
00:33:55,660 --> 00:33:58,460
And soon, Goering faced another problem.

437
00:33:59,900 --> 00:34:02,360
Allied bombers were so successful at targeting airfields

438
00:34:03,100 --> 00:34:04,960
that Germany was running out of runways.

439
00:34:09,760 --> 00:34:13,600
Hermann Göring realized that he needed a different kind of flying machine,

440
00:34:13,601 --> 00:34:16,480
one that could be hidden in the woods, hidden in the backyard,

441
00:34:16,840 --> 00:34:18,920
hidden anywhere, that could take off vertically,

442
00:34:19,620 --> 00:34:21,960
make a transition to horizontal flight,

443
00:34:23,100 --> 00:34:25,820
come back after its mission of attacking the B-17 bombers,

444
00:34:26,540 --> 00:34:29,260
make a transition, and land vertically.

445
00:34:29,560 --> 00:34:30,780
It did not need a runway.

446
00:34:33,540 --> 00:34:36,300
Astonishingly, Gehring turned to a zoology professor

447
00:34:36,301 --> 00:34:40,100
from Gothenum University, Eric von Holz, for a solution.

448
00:34:40,101 --> 00:34:46,540
Von Holst was an eccentric professor with a lifelong passion for dragonflies and hummingbirds.

449
00:34:47,080 --> 00:34:52,460
He was fascinated with the way they flew, particularly how they took off vertically and flew horizontally.

450
00:34:53,620 --> 00:34:59,480
He believed it might be possible to apply these principles of nature to a man-made flying machine.

451
00:35:01,900 --> 00:35:08,000
Using paper and rubber bands, he designed and built models that replicated the flight characteristics of the dragonfly.

452
00:35:10,100 --> 00:35:15,780
His paper, D. Treepflugel, the powered wing, proposed a vertical takeoff plane.

453
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:21,380
Such a design was long sought after in the aviation world,

454
00:35:21,880 --> 00:35:26,860
and Fucke-Wulf, the aircraft engineering company, and many others, thought it might work.

455
00:35:27,620 --> 00:35:31,780
To Hermann Göring in 1944, it looked like a perfect solution.

456
00:35:34,240 --> 00:35:37,600
The Treepflugel would have stood 30 foot high.

457
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:45,340
Its fuselage would be surrounded by three 30-foot-long rotors.

458
00:35:46,100 --> 00:35:48,620
At each tip would be a two-foot ramjet engine.

459
00:35:52,260 --> 00:35:56,260
When ignited, the ramjets would lift the machine off the ground.

460
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:03,840
Once airborne, it would turn gradually until it was in a high-speed horizontal flight.

461
00:36:05,560 --> 00:36:08,740
The Treepflugel would then be in position to attack.

462
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:20,240
It would rise up to their flying altitude of, say, 28,000 feet and meet them head-on and

463
00:36:20,241 --> 00:36:25,840
fly through the entire bomber pack, hoping to destroy bombers or perhaps to disable bombers,

464
00:36:25,841 --> 00:36:31,400
and then later on Messerschmitt BF-109s and Focke-Wulf 190s could finish him off.

465
00:36:32,840 --> 00:36:37,000
To land, the Treepflugel would return to vertical flight.

466
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:42,240
But it remained just an idea.

467
00:36:42,740 --> 00:36:45,300
The war had ended before it could ever come to fruition.

468
00:36:45,440 --> 00:36:47,020
And I don't really think it would ever have worked.

469
00:36:47,140 --> 00:36:50,300
That's why I'd nominate that for the weirdest weapon of the lot.

470
00:36:51,860 --> 00:36:56,100
But something just as weird was being cooked up halfway across the world.

471
00:36:57,140 --> 00:36:59,020
Germany's greatest ally, Japan,

472
00:36:59,021 --> 00:37:03,020
was working out how to turn radio waves into a death ray.

473
00:37:03,021 --> 00:37:13,660
In the 1930s, scientists in every major country in the world

474
00:37:13,661 --> 00:37:16,480
were looking to see if radio waves could be used as a weapon.

475
00:37:19,720 --> 00:37:23,140
One popular idea was to turn radio waves into a death ray.

476
00:37:24,980 --> 00:37:27,860
It was already a staple ingredient of science fiction films.

477
00:37:28,660 --> 00:37:31,380
The Japanese in particular were keen to develop the weapon.

478
00:37:31,381 --> 00:37:35,600
Well, it's interesting the Japanese pursued this technology.

479
00:37:35,601 --> 00:37:39,580
It was a weapon that was considered science fiction for the time,

480
00:37:39,780 --> 00:37:43,120
and only the Japanese really took it seriously enough

481
00:37:43,121 --> 00:37:46,560
to have dedicated programs for development of a death ray.

482
00:37:47,280 --> 00:37:51,900
But after extensive research, scientists in Japan put the idea on hold.

483
00:37:52,540 --> 00:37:55,900
They realized that technology was not yet sufficiently advanced.

484
00:37:56,980 --> 00:37:59,800
The bottom line was the power source.

485
00:38:00,660 --> 00:38:07,060
They simply could not generate microwaves in sufficient strength at that time for a feasible weapon.

486
00:38:07,900 --> 00:38:10,940
While the Japanese had been getting nowhere with death rays,

487
00:38:11,440 --> 00:38:12,254
British and U.S.

488
00:38:12,255 --> 00:38:16,220
scientists had made a vitally important breakthrough using radio waves, radar.

489
00:38:20,640 --> 00:38:22,940
The advantages of radar were obvious.

490
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:29,140
A system that could give advance warning of attacking aircraft on their way would be an invaluable defense.

491
00:38:29,800 --> 00:38:37,520
Obvious though it was, not everyone saw the benefits right away.

492
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:42,320
Japanese military leaders, still hankering after some kind of death ray,

493
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:45,740
were more interested in a lethal weapon than radar.

494
00:38:46,860 --> 00:38:52,040
Even though the scientists and engineers provided them technology that could be used,

495
00:38:52,041 --> 00:38:57,320
the military services did not express much interest in developing radar.

496
00:38:58,180 --> 00:39:03,360
The Navy, for example, saw radar largely useful as a navigational aid.

497
00:39:04,780 --> 00:39:07,620
When Japan first entered the war in 1941,

498
00:39:08,340 --> 00:39:12,420
their forces swept through the Pacific, easily overcoming Allied resistance.

499
00:39:13,280 --> 00:39:16,420
They had, they thought, all the weapons they needed.

500
00:39:19,700 --> 00:39:25,360
Then, in June 1942, the Japanese suffered their first major setback.

501
00:39:27,320 --> 00:39:32,720
When American and Japanese ships went head to head in one of the great sea battles of World War II.

502
00:39:35,960 --> 00:39:43,080
Against overwhelming odds, the American Navy won a great victory in what came to be known as the Battle of Midway.

503
00:39:45,900 --> 00:39:49,600
The Japanese were stunned from the results of Midway.

504
00:39:50,680 --> 00:39:54,260
Many of their prime ships lay at the bottom of the North Pacific.

505
00:39:54,261 --> 00:40:00,240
But psychologically, the Japanese Navy has suffered its first major defeat.

506
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:09,100
Desperate, with defeat looming, the Japanese high command appealed to its scientists and engineers for ideas for new weapons.

507
00:40:10,140 --> 00:40:16,880
A brilliant young naval scientist called Ito Yoji had two extraordinary suggestions for Japan's leaders to consider.

508
00:40:17,680 --> 00:40:23,160
Either build an atomic bomb or reinvestigate the idea of a death ray.

509
00:40:24,160 --> 00:40:27,480
After months and months of research on the atomic bomb,

510
00:40:27,880 --> 00:40:31,040
they found it simply wasn't feasible to build a nuclear weapon.

511
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:33,000
So they went with the death ray.

512
00:40:34,320 --> 00:40:38,560
Ironically, the success of radar in Britain had led to a technological breakthrough

513
00:40:38,561 --> 00:40:42,360
which might now help the Japanese in their pursuit of a death ray.

514
00:40:43,720 --> 00:40:48,420
At the outbreak of war, Britain had a coastal radar system called Chain Home.

515
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:52,340
It worked well, but because it used long radio waves,

516
00:40:52,341 --> 00:40:57,060
it required radio masts 400 feet high to send and receive the signal.

517
00:40:58,020 --> 00:41:02,160
In 1940, two British scientists from the University of Birmingham,

518
00:41:02,480 --> 00:41:03,940
Henry Boot and John Randall,

519
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:07,080
invented a device called a cavity magnetron.

520
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:10,700
It could produce much more power in much shorter wavelengths.

521
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:13,140
They were called microwaves.

522
00:41:15,360 --> 00:41:19,700
Microwaves are a matter of a fraction of an inch in wavelength

523
00:41:19,701 --> 00:41:22,760
and convey a lot of heat energy into a small space.

524
00:41:23,080 --> 00:41:27,360
That's why it is that we all have microwave ovens in our homes.

525
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:32,000
Microwave technology meant that smaller radar transmitters

526
00:41:32,001 --> 00:41:33,780
could now be carried on ships and planes.

527
00:41:34,540 --> 00:41:38,140
It also had a practical application for Japan's killer ray gun.

528
00:41:39,940 --> 00:41:42,700
The reason that the invention of the magnetron

529
00:41:42,701 --> 00:41:44,840
seemed to bring the death ray closer

530
00:41:44,841 --> 00:41:49,380
is that microwaves carry more energy than conventional radio waves do.

531
00:41:49,700 --> 00:41:54,060
You can be bombarded with radio waves all day from a certain distance.

532
00:41:54,140 --> 00:41:55,340
It's not going to kill you.

533
00:41:55,580 --> 00:42:00,160
But you can't be bombarded with microwaves for very long without it damaging.

534
00:42:01,820 --> 00:42:07,360
Using microwave technology, the Japanese Army built extraordinary death ray prototypes

535
00:42:07,361 --> 00:42:11,880
at a new laboratory in Shimada, 100 miles west of Tokyo.

536
00:42:14,620 --> 00:42:17,460
They looked much like a modern-day satellite dish.

537
00:42:18,460 --> 00:42:22,400
They would send out a strong, highly focused beam of microwaves,

538
00:42:22,800 --> 00:42:24,260
which would be fired at a target.

539
00:42:28,740 --> 00:42:34,680
What we have is a device that you can point at an invading army

540
00:42:34,681 --> 00:42:39,600
and one sweeping stroke fry all the people who are coming at you.

541
00:42:39,880 --> 00:42:43,520
Skin melting, eyes melting, people dropping dead.

542
00:42:43,521 --> 00:42:48,900
it would, in essence, cook them from within.

543
00:42:49,260 --> 00:42:50,700
It would denature their protein.

544
00:42:51,260 --> 00:42:54,440
It would heat up their cells so that they could no longer function.

545
00:42:55,740 --> 00:42:59,720
Japanese army records show that the first death ray victims were rabbits.

546
00:43:01,940 --> 00:43:04,160
As the death rays got more powerful,

547
00:43:04,820 --> 00:43:07,800
Japanese records state that they used monkeys as targets.

548
00:43:08,600 --> 00:43:11,840
And that has a sinister implication

549
00:43:11,841 --> 00:43:17,080
because many times, instead of recording human beings, they would write monkeys.

550
00:43:17,420 --> 00:43:23,600
So it's a possibility that the Army also used human beings in this research with a death ray.

551
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:29,180
We don't know that for sure, but there's a very high probability that they did.

552
00:43:30,100 --> 00:43:33,700
But killing animals and possibly humans in controlled conditions

553
00:43:33,701 --> 00:43:37,080
was a long way from producing a death ray for the battlefield.

554
00:43:37,081 --> 00:43:46,920
what you've got to do is make a weapon that can bring down a person a long way away and that's

555
00:43:46,921 --> 00:43:52,980
very different from just bombarding a sitting duck with microwaves the japanese hope to sight

556
00:43:52,981 --> 00:43:57,660
death rays on ships and along the coast to zap invading armies and bring down planes

557
00:43:57,661 --> 00:44:04,420
in tests they found a death ray could disable moving vehicles by short-circuiting the electrics

558
00:44:04,421 --> 00:44:07,100
but only from a distance of a few yards.

559
00:44:10,060 --> 00:44:12,920
So could the Japanese really have fringed the coast

560
00:44:12,921 --> 00:44:16,020
with microwave transmitters and stopped anything from coming there?

561
00:44:16,400 --> 00:44:17,260
Of course they couldn't.

562
00:44:17,580 --> 00:44:19,800
The amount of energy, in theory, you'd need to generate

563
00:44:19,801 --> 00:44:21,920
would be all the world's power stations put together.

564
00:44:23,280 --> 00:44:27,080
The war ended before the Japanese death ray was ready for battle.

565
00:44:27,840 --> 00:44:32,400
As Japanese leaders signed the formal surrender on September 2, 1945,

566
00:44:32,401 --> 00:44:37,420
they may well have concluded that if they'd chosen to concentrate their research

567
00:44:37,421 --> 00:44:40,640
on the weirdest weapon of them all, the nuclear bomb,

568
00:44:41,340 --> 00:44:44,800
then the outcome of World War II may have been very different.

569
00:45:02,400 --> 00:45:03,400
Let's go find out.

570
00:45:03,760 --> 00:45:04,720
Digging for the truth.

571
00:45:05,020 --> 00:45:06,040
The Da Vinci Code.

572
00:45:06,660 --> 00:45:07,360
Bloodlines.

573
00:45:07,400 --> 00:45:09,680
Tomorrow night at 9 on the History Channel.

